articles.content_en:
The Standing Committee of the National People's Congress has discussed the meaning of the circumstance "who has the ability to enforce a judgment or ruling of a People's Court but refuses to do so, and the circumstances are serious" as provided in Article 313 of the Criminal Law, and hereby interprets as follows:
The "judgments and rulings of a People's Court" as provided in Article 313 of the Criminal Law refer to judgments and rulings that are lawfully made by a People's Court, have enforcement content, and have already taken legal effect. The rulings made by a People's Court for the lawful enforcement of payment orders, effective mediation documents, arbitration awards, notarized creditor's rights documents, etc., shall fall within the scope of rulings under that Article.
The following circumstances shall fall within the circumstance "who has the ability to enforce but refuses to do so, and the circumstances are serious" as provided in Article 313 of the Criminal Law:
(1) the person subject to enforcement conceals, transfers, intentionally damages property, or transfers property without compensation, or transfers property at a price obviously unreasonably low, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(2) the surety or the person subject to enforcement conceals, transfers, intentionally damages, or transfers the property that has been provided as security to the People's Court, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(3) the person obligated to assist in enforcement, upon receiving a notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, refuses to assist in enforcement, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(4) the person subject to enforcement, the surety, or the person obligated to assist in enforcement colludes with a functionary of a State organ, and uses the functionary's powers to obstruct enforcement, thereby rendering the judgment or ruling unenforceable;
(5) other circumstances where the person has the ability to enforce but refuses to do so, and the circumstances are serious.
If a functionary of a State organ commits the act described in item (4) above, he or she shall be investigated for criminal liability as an accomplice to the crime of refusing to enforce a judgment or ruling. If a functionary of a State organ commits the act described in item (4) above by accepting bribes or abusing his or her powers, and such act also constitutes a crime under Article 385 or Article 397 of the Criminal Law, he or she shall be convicted and punished in accordance with the provision imposing the heavier penalty.
This interpretation is hereby announced.
